course: everyday
lesson: 456
title: The bulldog next door who never likes you
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Every morning Sam walks past number 14, and every morning Tank the bulldog throws himself at the gate, growling like a lawnmower that hates you personally. Sam tries biscuits, a squeaky ball, even a baby voice. Tank eats the biscuits and growls through the crumbs. Mr Doyle, his owner, leans out: «Don't feed him, son. He'll be sick and he'll still not like you.»
explain: Tank isn't wicked. He was a rescue: someone kept him shut in a shed for two years, so now anyone near his gate is trouble as far as he's concerned. Fear makes him guard hard. When Jake rattles a stick along the railings «to wind the mutt up», Tank slips his collar a week later and chases Jake to the corner. Jake isn't bitten, but he's white as a sheet, and it's the Doyles who get the warning letter from the council, not Jake.
explain: Kieran dares Sam: «Go on, climb the fence and pat him, he's all bark innit.» Sam's hand is on the top bar when Mr Doyle's door bangs open: «Get DOWN!» Tank hits the fence a second after Sam jumps back. There's a tooth hole in Sam's jumper sleeve. Sam's legs go like jelly. Later Kieran's big brother says bulldogs' jaws «lock» once they bite; Lily's mum looks it up with her — that's a myth, but any dog bite is serious and needs a grown-up and often a doctor.
explain: Sam's dad teaches the real rule for a dog running at you: stand still like a tree, arms in, look away. Staring a dog down, like Connor's uncle says, is a challenge. Mr Doyle says Tank is soft as butter with him and rolls over for belly rubs — but when Maddie rolls over on the pavement herself to copy the trick, Tank only growls harder: his trust in Mr Doyle doesn't pass to strangers. When Lily drops her ice cream cone through the Doyles' fence, she knocks and asks Mr Doyle to fetch it. Tank and Biscuit, the pug from across the road, bark at each other on every walk, so their owners now walk them at different times. Mr Doyle offers Sam a proper meeting, with Sam's mum there. Tank sniffs his hand, grumbles and lies down. «That's a yes from him,» says Mr Doyle. A year on, Tank still growls every morning. Sam just says «Alright, Tank» and walks on. Some dogs never come round — and that's not your fault.
fact: A dog guarding its own garden or gate is doing a job; its growling is not about you personally.
fact: Dogs that were frightened or badly treated when young often stay fearful and guard hard for the rest of their lives.
fact: Feeding someone else's dog without asking can make it ill and does not make it like you.
fact: Never climb into a dog's garden or yard, not for a dare, a ball or a dropped ice cream: knock and ask the owner.
fact: Dogs that bark a lot can also bite; «all bark and no bite» is not a safe rule.
fact: Teasing a dog through a fence makes it more dangerous for everyone, and the dog and its owners are the ones who get blamed.
fact: If a dog runs at you, stand still like a tree, keep your arms in and look away; staring at it is a challenge.
fact: The idea that bulldogs' jaws «lock» shut is a myth, but any dog bite is serious and needs an adult and often a doctor.
fact: A dog can be gentle with its own family and fierce with strangers; the owner's trust does not pass to you.
fact: The safe way to meet a dog is with the owner holding it, your parent knowing, and the dog choosing how close to come.
fact: Some dogs never warm up to you however kind you are, and you can still be good neighbours from a distance.
fact: Two dogs that hate each other are often walked at different times; keeping them apart is a sensible answer.
fact: «Innit» is London and southern English slang tagged on the end, meaning roughly «isn't it» or «right?».
exercise: Why does Tank throw himself at the gate when Sam walks past? | He is guarding his family's garden | He wants Sam to throw him a ball to chase | He is trying to get a biscuit | He thinks Sam is his owner
exercise: What did Mr Doyle say about feeding Tank biscuits? | He'd be sick and still not like Sam | It would make him friends for life | Only chocolate biscuits work on him | He'd stop growling after ten biscuits
exercise: Why does Tank guard so fiercely? | He was kept in a shed and is still afraid | He was trained by the Doyles to bite children | Bulldogs are born hating people | He is angry he is not fed enough
exercise: Kieran dared Sam to climb the fence. What nearly happened? | Tank reached him and tore his sleeve | Tank licked his hand and wagged | Mr Doyle gave him a biscuit to share | Nothing, Tank was asleep indoors
exercise: Kieran said Tank is «all bark». Was that a safe rule? | No, dogs that bark a lot can bite too | Yes, a dog that barks a lot never bites | Yes, but only for bulldogs | No, only quiet dogs ever bark
exercise: Jake rattled a stick on the railings. What came of it? | Tank got out and chased him, and the Doyles got a warning | Tank got used to the noise and stopped barking at everyone | Jake was given a medal by the council | Mr Doyle thanked Jake for training him
exercise: Who usually pays when a teased dog finally gets out? | The dog and its owners | Only the person who teased it | Nobody, dogs are never blamed | The postman on that street
exercise: A loose dog runs at you in the park. What should you do? | Stand still, arms in, look away | Stare it straight in the eye | Run away as fast as you can | Wave your arms and shout at it loudly
exercise: Kieran's brother says bulldogs' jaws lock when they bite. Is that true? | No, it's a myth, but any bite is serious | Yes, they stay locked until the dog falls asleep | Yes, but only on Tuesdays | No, bulldogs have no teeth at all
exercise: Maddie rolled over on the pavement to copy Tank's belly rubs. Why didn't it work? | Tank's trust in Mr Doyle doesn't pass to strangers | Maddie rolled the wrong way round, so Tank was confused | Tank only likes people in uniform | Tank was too sleepy to notice
exercise: Lily dropped her ice cream cone through the Doyles' fence. What's the safe move? | Knock and ask Mr Doyle to fetch it | Nip in quickly while Tank isn't looking | Climb over and grab it with a stick | Send a smaller kid in to get it for you
exercise: How did Sam meet Tank properly? | With Mr Doyle holding him and Sam's mum there | Alone, after climbing the fence | By sneaking in at night with a pocketful of ham | By shouting Tank's name at the gate
exercise: What did «a yes from him» mean when Tank sniffed and lay down? | Not growling was as friendly as Tank gets | Tank was about to attack | Tank wanted Sam to take him home with him | Tank had fallen ill from eating biscuits
exercise: Tank and the pug Biscuit bark at each other every walk. What did the owners do? | Walked them at different times | Made them sleep in the same basket | Let them fight it out once | Gave both dogs away
exercise: A year later Tank still growls at Sam. Whose fault is that? | Nobody's, some dogs never warm up | Sam's, he didn't try enough biscuits | Mr Doyle's, for keeping a dog | The council's, for the letter
exercise: «He's all bark innit». What does «innit» add here? | «Right?» or «isn't it» | «Run away now» | «He is very old» | «I'm not sure at all»
test: «bruv just lob him a sausage every day, he'll love u by friday». Will that work? | No, it won't make him like you and may make him ill | Yes, a sausage a day beats any growl, every time | Yes, but it takes exactly a week | No, bulldogs only eat cat food
test: You're on the top bar of the fence and Tank is running at you. What would most likely happen if you jumped in? | You'd likely be bitten | He'd stop and wag his tail at you | He'd run off scared of you | Mr Doyle would be pleased
test: Connor: «my uncle says u stare it down, show it who's boss». Who's right, Connor or Sam's dad? | Sam's dad: stand still and look away | Connor: stare it down hard till it backs off | Both, stare then run | Neither, lie flat and scream
test: «it's only a dog, a bite's nothing, just wash it». True? | No, a dog bite needs an adult and often a doctor | Yes, bites heal by themselves if you keep them clean | Yes, if the dog is small | No, you need to bite the dog back
test: In a cartoon, the hero tickles a growling guard dog and it turns into a puppy. What's wrong with that? | Real guard dogs may bite a stranger who reaches in | Nothing, tickling always calms dogs | Dogs can't hear anything at all while they're growling | Only cartoon dogs ever growl
test: Jake: «proper mental, that mutt, he went for me for no reason». Is that fair? | No, Jake had been teasing him with a stick | Yes, Tank is just a bad dog | Yes, bulldogs often attack people for no reason | No, Tank was chasing a cat
test: If Mr Doyle hadn't come out when Sam was on the fence, what would most likely have happened? | Tank would have got hold of Sam | Tank would have let Sam stroke him | Tank would have gone back indoors to sleep | Kieran would have pulled Sam to safety
test: Mr Doyle says Tank is soft with him and rolls over for belly rubs. What does that tell you? | A dog can be gentle with its family and fierce with others | Tank will roll over for anyone who rubs his belly the right way | Tank is only pretending to growl | Belly rubs work on all dogs
test: Sam tried everything and Tank still growls a year later. What's the grown-up way to see it? | Not every dog will like you, and that's alright | Sam must be doing something wrong | Tank will suddenly change one day | Sam should keep feeding him till he does
test: Lily won't walk down the street for a week because of the «locking jaw» story. What helps most? | Checking the story with a grown-up and keeping a safe distance | Never leaving the house again | Testing it on Tank herself, from just behind the gate | Believing everything big brothers say
test: «Get DOWN!» Mr Doyle yells at Sam on the fence. Is he being mean? | No, he's stopping Sam getting bitten | Yes, he hates children, especially Sam | Yes, he wanted Tank to get hold of Sam | No, he was shouting at the dog, not at Sam
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a guard dog exhibits aggression towards persons who linger at its gate. | It perceives such persons as a threat to the premises it defends | It harbours a personal animosity towards each individual | It seeks to obtain confectionery from passers-by | It has been instructed to attack all children
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the assertion that a dog which barks persistently will not bite? | It is unreliable, since such dogs may also bite | It is a scientific law without exception | It applies to all breeds save bulldogs | It is true provided the dog is fed beforehand
exam: Given that a loose dog approaches a child at speed, ascertain the conduct most likely to avert injury. | Remaining motionless with arms lowered and gaze averted | Maintaining a fixed stare until the animal at last retreats | Running at maximum speed in the opposite direction | Waving the arms and shouting loudly
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the belief that the jaws of a bulldog lock upon biting. | It is a myth, though any dog bite is to be treated as serious | It is established fact requiring no medical attention | It is true solely of dogs rescued from sheds | It is a myth, and dog bites are invariably trivial
